{
  "id": "cond",
  "label": "Conditions — the six types",
  "short": "Conditions",
  "taughtAt": 33,
  "layout": {
    "rowLabels": [
      "Simple present",
      "Simple past",
      "Future more vivid",
      "Future less vivid",
      "Present contrary to fact",
      "Past contrary to fact"
    ],
    "colLabels": [
      "protasis (sī…)",
      "apodosis (then…)"
    ]
  },
  "notes": "Two questions settle every condition: is the verb INDICATIVE or SUBJUNCTIVE (fact vs. supposition), and WHICH tense? Indicative = the three plain types; subjunctive = should-would (present), contrary to fact now (imperfect), contrary to fact then (pluperfect). Negative protasis: nisi.",
  "cells": [
